-- 048: the sentence explaining the table contradicted the table.
--
-- 047 introduced a rule for the profile column and then miscounted its own
-- output: "Two of the twelve roles clear that bar on two dimensions" when
-- three do, which any reader can check in five seconds against the table
-- four lines above it. All five translators counted independently and all
-- five reported it.
--
-- The worked example was also not an example of the rule it illustrated.
-- The clause is "a role anchored on one plane can still show a dimension
-- from the other", and Bee draws Bond from the social plane and Vision from
-- the cognitive one, so Bee is not anchored on a single plane at all. Owl
-- is: Presence and Bond are both social, and its Discipline comes from the
-- other plane. The Danish translator worked that out and named the right
-- replacement.
--
-- Both claims in the new sentence are now asserted against role-scoring.js
-- in the generator, so the article cannot ship disagreeing with the data
-- again.

BEGIN;

UPDATE blog_posts SET
  content    = jsonb_set(content, '{en}', '"# The 12 Cèrcol team roles explained: which animal are you?\n\nCèrcol maps Big Five personality profiles onto 12 team roles. Each role is named after an animal. The naming is deliberate: animals are memorable, culturally neutral, carry no hierarchy, and resist the kind of status signalling that plagues role systems where some types are implicitly more desirable than others.\n\nThis article explains why Cèrcol uses the role system, what its scientific foundations are, how to read the 12 roles and, critically, what the roles are not.\n\n## Why Animal Names Resist Status Hierarchies\n\nPersonality typologies have a long history of role names that carry implicit rankings. \"Visionary,\" \"Strategist,\" and \"Driver\" sound more important than \"Supporter,\" \"Analyst,\" or \"Implementer\", regardless of how the documentation insists they are all equally valuable. Whether a label''s connotations change how a team uses it is not something Cèrcol has measured, but the risk is cheap to avoid, and avoiding it is why the labels are animals rather than job titles.\n\nAnimal names sidestep this problem. An Owl is not intrinsically more valuable than a Bee. The names are easy to remember, easy to say in conversation, and carry enough associative meaning to be useful without implying rank.\n\nThat is what the naming is optimising for: roles a team treats as genuinely equal rather than as a disguised prestige hierarchy. Whether it succeeds is an empirical question Cèrcol has not yet answered, and it is one the accumulating data should eventually settle.\n\n## The AB5C Circumplex: The Science Behind the 12 Roles\n\nThe 12 roles are derived from the **Abridged Big Five Circumplex (AB5C)**, a framework developed by Hofstee, De Raad, and Goldberg (1992, DOI: 10.1037/0022-3514.63.1.146). The circumplex positions personality traits in a two-dimensional space defined by a pair of Big Five factors. Extraversion and Agreeableness define one such plane, for instance, and Openness and Conscientiousness define another.\n\nThe key insight of the circumplex model is that traits are not independent points but occupy positions in a circular arrangement. \"Dominant\" sits between high Extraversion and low Agreeableness. \"Cooperative\" sits between high Agreeableness and moderate Extraversion. The angle in the circumplex is often as informative as the distance from the centre.\n\nThe AB5C defines a circumplex for each of the ten possible pairs of factors. Cèrcol anchors its 12 roles in two of them:\n\n- The **social balance** plane (Presence × Bond, i.e., Extraversion × Agreeableness): captures how a person contributes to the social dynamics of the team\n- The **cognitive balance** plane (Vision × Discipline, i.e., Openness × Conscientiousness): captures how a person processes information and approaches work\n\nThe fifth dimension, Depth (Neuroticism), modulates the expression of the other four, it affects intensity, emotional range, and responsiveness to stress, and is reflected in each role description without defining a separate axis.\n\n## The 12 Cèrcol Team Roles at a Glance\n\n\n<figure>\n<svg class=\"diagram\" viewBox=\"0 0 500 340\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <!-- Pentagon background -->\n  <polygon points=\"250,30 452,175 374,320 126,320 48,175\"\n           fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0047ba\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <!-- Pentagon sides faint fill -->\n  <polygon points=\"250,30 452,175 374,320 126,320 48,175\"\n           fill=\"#0047ba\" fill-opacity=\"0.04\"/>\n\n  <!-- Vertex dots -->\n  <circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"30\"  r=\"10\" fill=\"#0047ba\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"452\" cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#427c42\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"374\" cy=\"320\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#cf3339\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"126\" cy=\"320\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#f1c22f\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"48\"  cy=\"175\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#0047ba\" fill-opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n\n  <!-- Vertex labels -->\n  <!-- Top: Openness -->\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#0047ba\">Openness</text>\n  <!-- Top-right: Conscientiousness -->\n  <text x=\"468\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#427c42\">Conscientiousness</text>\n  <!-- Bottom-right: Extraversion -->\n  <text x=\"390\" y=\"338\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#cf3339\">Extraversion</text>\n  <!-- Bottom-left: Agreeableness -->\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"338\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#f1c22f\" stroke=\"#c8991a\" stroke-width=\"0.3\">Agreeableness</text>\n  <!-- Top-left: Neuroticism -->\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#0047ba\" fill-opacity=\"0.75\">Neuroticism</text>\n\n  <!-- Centre label -->\n  <rect x=\"165\" y=\"150\" width=\"170\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"white\" stroke=\"#0047ba\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0.95\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"167\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#333\">12 Cèrcol Roles</text>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#666\">mapped to the Big Five</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>The five Big Five factors Cèrcol scores. Four of them separate the roles from each other; Neuroticism, which Cèrcol calls Depth, shifts how intensely a role is expressed rather than which role it is.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n| Role | Profile | What it gives the team | Blind spot |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dolphin | Presence+, Bond+ | Lowers the social activation energy; people open up | The productive friction that sharpens an idea |\n| Wolf | Presence+, Bond-, Discipline+ | Contests weak ideas and raises the standard | The relational cost of being right |\n| Elephant | Presence-, Bond+ | Holds the group''s memory and trust without taking space | Goes quiet exactly when it should speak |\n| Owl | Presence-, Bond-, Discipline+ | Independent rigour, needs no audience | Its work never reaching whoever decides |\n| Eagle | Presence+, Vision+ | Opens new directions and can sell them | The execution detail that would make them real |\n| Falcon | Presence+, Vision-, Discipline+ | Turns a decision into movement, now | Discards the unconventional option that turns out to have been right |\n| Octopus | Presence-, Vision+, Discipline- | Connects ideas nobody had put together | Closing; moves on to the next idea |\n| Tortoise | Presence-, Vision-, Discipline+ | Finishes what it starts, unsupervised | The signal that the route needs to change |\n| Bee | Bond+, Vision+, Discipline+ | Coordinates people and work at the same time | Absorbs everyone else''s coordination load |\n| Bear | Bond+, Vision-, Discipline+ | Stability and trust when everything is moving | Shields the group from change it needs |\n| Fox | Bond-, Vision+, Discipline- | Spots the opening before anyone else | The sustained commitment after spotting it |\n| Badger | Bond-, Vision-, Discipline+ | Defends the standard even when it annoys | That the standard may no longer be right |\n\nThe profile column lists each of the four role-separating dimensions whose centroid sits at least half a standard deviation from the population average. Three of the twelve roles clear that bar on two dimensions and the other nine on three, which is why a role anchored on one plane can still show a dimension from the other: Owl is anchored on Presence and Bond, both on the social plane, and its Discipline is far enough from average to name.\nA plus means the role sits high on that dimension relative to the\npopulation, a minus means low. Depth is deliberately absent: it shifts how\nintensely a role is expressed but it does not separate one role from\nanother, so putting it in this table would invite reading it as\ndistinctive when it is not.\n\nThese descriptions are anchors, not full portraits. Each role has a facet-level description in the Cèrcol [roles documentation](/roles) that shows how the role is expressed across the six facets of each relevant dimension.\n\n## How to Read Your Team Map: Balance, Gaps, and Complements\n\nThe Cèrcol team map places each team member at their role position in the profile space. Reading it well requires looking at the distribution rather than at individual points.\n\n**Balance vs concentration.** A team where most members cluster around Eagle and Fox has energy and no shortage of ideas, and it will probably struggle with sustained execution, process maintenance and interpersonal repair. A team clustered around Tortoise and Bear is reliable and cohesive, and it may move slowly on anything novel, or avoid a conflict it needs to have.\n\n**Complementary pairs.** Some roles are natural complements that generate productive tension when paired. A Wolf and a Dolphin together produce both rigorous critique and strong relational repair, but they need a shared understanding of when each mode is appropriate. Without it the pairing turns into conflict rather than complement.\n\n**Structural gaps.** A team with no Owl may consistently underweight evidence and rigour. A team with no Bee may have strong individual performers who cannot coordinate. Gaps on the team map are not problems to solve immediately, but they are worth naming, because an unnamed gap generates friction nobody can explain.\n\nOnce you understand your own role, peer feedback from colleagues through [what the Cèrcol Witness instrument measures](/blog/what-the-cercol-witness-instrument-measures) will show whether others experience you the same way. And if you want to put this to work immediately, [using Cèrcol for team development](/blog/using-cercol-for-team-development-a-practical-guide) walks you through the full facilitation cycle step by step.\n\nFor the science behind personality composition at the team level, see [does personality composition predict team performance?](/blog/does-personality-composition-predict-team-performance), and for context on what underlies each dimension score, [how personality test scores are calculated](/blog/how-personality-test-scores-are-calculated) is a useful companion.\n\n> \"The team map does not tell you who the right people are. It tells you what patterns of tendency are present, and absent. The most valuable conversations happen when a team looks at its own map and recognises something it already knew but had never said.\"\n\n## What the Roles Are Not: Types, Ranks, or Fixed Identities\n\nThis is as important as understanding what the roles are.\n\n**They are not types.** The roles are not boxes that people permanently inhabit. They are positions in a continuous, multi-dimensional space. Two people with the same role may be adjacent rather than identical in their full profiles, and they will experience their role differently as a result.\n\n**They are not fixed.** [Big Five](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Five_personality_traits) traits are relatively stable in adulthood, but they do shift, particularly during major life transitions and over decades (Roberts et al., 2006, DOI: 10.1037/0033-2909.132.1.1). Someone who read as an Eagle in their twenties may read closer to a Bee as they build expertise and take on more structured leadership. The Cèrcol role is a current profile, not a permanent identity.\n\n**They are not ranked.** No role is more valuable, more intelligent, more suited to leadership, or more desirable than another. Any team that begins to treat certain roles as more prestigious is misusing the framework. The system is explicitly designed, through the animal naming, the visual presentation, and the documentation, to prevent this.\n\n**They are not destiny.** A person with a Wolf profile is not doomed to create conflict. A person with a Tortoise profile is not incapable of strategic thinking. The role describes statistical tendencies, not a ceiling.\n\n## Using Roles in Practice: A Shared Vocabulary for Teams\n\nThe roles are most useful as a shared vocabulary for conversations the team was already having, imprecisely, often frustratingly, without common language. \"this project needs more Bee than it has\" is a more actionable, less personal observation than \"you need to communicate better.\"\n\nFor the [science behind the role system](/science), including the circumplex methodology and psychometric validation, see the Cèrcol science documentation. For practical application in team composition, see the [roles page](/roles). For a structured workshop format, see [how to run a team personality workshop](/blog/how-to-run-a-team-personality-workshop).\n\n## Explore your team map on Cèrcol, free\n\nYour role profile is one data point. The real insight comes when you layer in how your colleagues actually experience you. Cèrcol gives you both: a [12-role team map](/roles) built from validated Big Five science, and the [Witness peer assessment](/instruments) that reveals the gap between self-perception and external reality. Take the free assessment at [cercol.team](https://cercol.team), invite your team, and see where your collective profile clusters, and where the gaps are.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [What the Cèrcol Witness instrument measures](/blog/what-the-cercol-witness-instrument-measures)\n- [Using Cèrcol for team development: a practical guide](/blog/using-cercol-for-team-development-a-practical-guide)\n- [How to run a team personality workshop](/blog/how-to-run-a-team-personality-workshop)\n- [Building a team from scratch: what personality data can and can''t tell you](/blog/building-a-team-from-scratch-what-personality-data-can-and-cant-tell-you)\n- [High-performing team structures: a personality perspective](/blog/high-performing-team-structures-personality-perspective)\n- [Personality diversity in technical teams](/blog/personality-diversity-in-technical-teams)"'::jsonb),
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'the-12-cercol-team-roles-explained';

COMMIT;
